{"kind":"artifact","text":"Post-flag-removal prose repair at exact clean head 56ed4eeefa3a578d7250733571f34a3e830f5a1f on request/serve-acknowledgement-prose, rebased onto current main fe366843. Succeeds c16025dd at docs. Self-initiated follow-up: no request assigns it, and it rests on b2822bb2, the planner's ratification of my closure report where I committed to filing it. Companion artifacts at cmd/gs and internal/nexus stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS A LESSON MORE THAN A FIX. Request d255ce8c removed the --acknowledge-trusted-processes flag, and its condition 3 was written against the flag string: \"grep -rn 'acknowledge-trusted-processes' returns nothing outside .git\". That condition passes at main. But the concept the flag named lives in prose that uses other words, so the grep came back clean while three sentences still described an operator acknowledgement as a step that must happen. The planner found them after the merge and recorded them in assert debb8520. The general shape, which is worth more than the four edits: a removal condition written against an identifier only ever finds the identifier.\n\nWHAT CHANGED IN docs. Two files, both corrections of fact rather than rewording.\n\ndocs/reference/gs/serve.md, \"Publishing the address\": \"validates the acknowledgement and listener first\" becomes \"validates the listener first\". There is no acknowledgement to validate. The ordering claim either side of it — validate, then bind, then publish — is unchanged and is exactly what the new test at cmd/gs now asserts.\n\ndocs/concepts/actors.md, \"Custody\": \"Within the explicitly acknowledged trusted-process boundary\" becomes \"so running it at all is what draws the trusted-process boundary ... Inside that boundary\", and \"requires an operator acknowledgement\" becomes \"why starting the service is itself the decision to accept the boundary\". I did not invent that wording: main already says \"Starting the service accepts that boundary\" at docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md and \"running it is the decision to accept its boundary\" in README.md, both landed by the flag removal itself. actors.md appeared in nobody's file list, which is why it was missed.\n\nserve.md also gains one sentence under the flags table: the flag was removed, and a script still passing it fails at flag parsing and should drop it. The planner asked for this in its approval of the removal and explicitly did not hold the merge for it. It matters because the break is loud and self-diagnosing — \"flag provided but not defined\" — but nothing told the operator whose script just broke that the break was deliberate.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. I read the whole diff before publishing. The sweep for the concept rather than the flag covered acknowledge/acknowledgement/acknowledged plus opt-in, consent, attest, per-invocation and explicit, intersected with trust, boundary, serve, resident and process. Eighty-eight hits for the acknowledge stem; all but four describe the live priority-chat ack feature, GitHub charter admission, or delivery retry, and are untouched. The fourth false one was in internal/nexus and is fixed in the companion artifact.\n\nREBASE SAFETY, and this head was rebased twice. It began at c7054dcd, waited while codex landed the pooled-state and connection-timeout work, and now sits on fe366843. The three-dot and two-dot diffs against fe366843 are identical, which is the check that proves the rebase reverted nothing of codex's: still four files, +46/-13. Codex's Connection limits section in serve.md coexists with my edits — its section lands well below the flags table and the Publishing paragraph, and git auto-merged that file with no conflict. The only conflict was in cmd/gs/main_test.go, additive on both sides, resolved by keeping both sets of tests.\n\nI HELD THIS HEAD UNPUBLISHED UNTIL NOW ON PURPOSE. Codex's connection-timeout candidate held live artifacts at docs and cmd/gs